       thread‐keyring - per‐thread keyring

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  thread  keyring  is  a  keyring used to anchor keys on behalf of a
       process.  It is created only when a thread  requests  it.   The  thread
       keyring has the name (description) ___t_i_d.

       A special serial number value, KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__TTHHRREEAADD__KKEEYYRRIINNGG, is defined that
       can be used in lieu of the actual serial number of the calling thread’s
       thread keyring.

       From  the  kkeeyyccttll(1) utility, ’@@tt’ can be used instead of a numeric key
       ID in much the same way, but as kkeeyyccttll(1) is a program run after  fork‐
       ing, this is of no utility.

       Thread  keyrings  are not inherited across cclloonnee(2) and ffoorrkk(2) and are
       cleared by eexxeeccvvee(2).  A thread keyring is destroyed  when  the  thread
       that refers to it terminates.

       Initially,  a  thread  does  not  have  a  thread keyring.  If a thread
       doesn’t have a thread keyring when it is accessed, then it will be cre‐
       ated if it is to be modified; otherwise the operation  fails  with  the
       error EENNOOKKEEYY.

